## Assignment

Study the reference, explain the product, prepare the evidence pack and PRD, then implement and verify a working application in the designated workspace. Carry out these phases in the same run as far as the environment allows. Begin implementation once you have enough evidence for the first end-to-end workflow, and continue researching unresolved areas as you build. If work spans sessions, keep a resumable record of completed work, verification, and remaining tasks.

## 1. Learn the product and inspect the reference

Read the workspace instructions, inspect the working tree, and preserve existing work. Confirm the workspace belongs to this project before writing application files. Read supplied references and search official public documentation when it helps resolve a concrete gap. If an approved reference requires login, let the user sign in directly through the browser or supply access through the approved mechanism. Keep credentials and session material out of prompts, files, captures, and the replacement app.

Map the product's purpose, owner or vendor where established, intended users, roles, and major business processes. Start with visible navigation and record every accessible module, route, tab, and nested screen. Inspect representative workflows across roles and layouts where available. Capture inputs, validation, tables, filters, actions, state changes, outputs, integrations, and visible error or empty states. Use synthetic records in an approved sandbox for actions that write data or trigger external effects. When production is the only reference, use read-only inspection and record untested behavior as unknown.

Collect useful screenshots, screen notes, and authorized frontend assets through supported tools. When permitted and available, inventory or collect browser-delivered HTML, CSS, JavaScript, fonts, icons, and images. Record each source and distinguish files actually collected from resources observed by name. Preserve applicable license notices, and sanitize screenshots and manifests for personal data, identifiers, credentials, and tokenized URLs. If public subdomain discovery is in scope, use passive public sources and record whether names were observed, visited, or unverified. Inspect only authorized pages and assets.

Use supplied source code, schemas, and official API documentation when authorized. Where tools and scope permit, document sanitized client-visible request and response shapes that support a workflow. Mark server logic, private API contracts, database schemas, and background jobs as unknown unless directly supported by authorized evidence.

Save a `reference-blueprint/` folder with a source index, navigation and module catalogue, screen and workflow notes, role and data observations, asset inventory, and a coverage ledger. Assign stable IDs to modules or workflows and record the source, role, date, depth inspected, and gaps. Label reference claims **Observed**, **Inferred**, or **Unknown**. Keep decisions for the replacement application distinct from evidence about the reference. If access is unavailable, document the specific gap and continue with the materials you have.

## 2. Write the PRD and implementation plan

Write `docs/product-understanding.md` with the product purpose, users, scope, major workflows, strongest evidence, and open questions. Create a separate `PRD/PRD.md` covering the problem, outcomes, roles, permissions, all scoped modules, functional requirements, user journeys, states, business rules, data, integrations, accessibility, security, reliability, priorities, and acceptance criteria. Link requirement IDs to evidence and identify proposed rules that need confirmation before production. Give each scoped module a concrete outcome or an explicit dependency.

Write `docs/implementation-roadmap.md` with the architecture choice, build sequence, dependencies, and progress. Use the PRD to define the new app and the blueprint to substantiate claims about the reference. Start building in the same run. Ask only about material gaps that block progress, and continue independent work while awaiting an answer. Use documented, configurable defaults for reversible choices.

## 3. Build the application

Use the existing stack where appropriate. For a new build, choose a maintainable stack suited to the requirements and document how to run it locally. Implement the shared navigation, visual system, authentication, server-side authorization, data isolation, and audit history wherever the PRD requires them. For features that need persistent state, include a database schema, migrations, and a repeatable synthetic seed. Provide an environment template with placeholders and a documented way to sign in as each demo role.

Deliver the highest-priority workflow as a complete slice from UI through validation, business logic, storage, and the resulting user-visible state. Then expand across the remaining modules. Reuse shared components and services while giving each module its own meaningful workflow. Implement validation, empty/loading/error states, keyboard access, responsive layouts, and record scoping where relevant. Use the reference and approved assets for fidelity. Make undocumented workflows configurable where their rules could vary.

Implement the configured integration scope. Where a live provider or approved credentials are unavailable, use a simulator or sandbox that exposes realistic outcomes and errors. Record which providers and actions are simulated. Keep live outbound messages, calls, charges, imports of real records, and public deployment within the user's explicit authorization.

## 4. Verify and hand off

Start the application using its documented setup and exercise the core journeys with synthetic data. Check that actions persist after refresh, connected modules reflect the same data, unauthorized roles are denied, and applicable tenant or account boundaries hold. Check invalid inputs and failure paths. Run applicable build, type, and existing test commands. Where the project calls for test coverage, include targeted regression cases and a manual acceptance checklist tied to requirement IDs. Record what ran, the outcomes, and any checks that could not run.

Maintain `docs/module-coverage.md` with each scoped module, route, linked requirements, available workflows, verification status, and gaps. Distinguish **Implemented**, **Simulated**, **Partial**, and **Pending**. A reachable page counts as navigation coverage. A feature counts as implemented when its primary action and resulting state work under the intended permissions. Continue until the agreed scope meets its acceptance criteria or a specific blocker prevents further progress.

Deliver the application source, `reference-blueprint/`, separate `PRD/`, a setup `README.md`, and a `HANDOFF.md` with architecture, demo roles, data setup, decisions, limitations, and remaining tasks. Provide a shareable handoff archive if requested, using only files approved for distribution. In the final summary, give the local start steps and report the exact module counts, verified journeys, simulated integrations, and any blocked or incomplete work. State the acceptance criteria still outstanding if delivery is partial.

